WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.385
This video uses the Genes and Sequences demo project.

00:00:07.385 --> 00:00:12.992
Choose Help, then Demo Projects, and click Download and Open beside it.

00:00:12.992 --> 00:00:18.441
It holds NG_000007.3, the human beta globin record from NCBI GenBank.

00:00:18.441 --> 00:00:21.995
The video Your first project shows each step.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.245
Click NG_000007.3 under Reference Sequences, then Focus.

00:00:27.245 --> 00:00:34.942
It holds the beta globin gene, which makes part of hemoglobin, the protein that carries oxygen in blood.

00:00:34.942 --> 00:00:38.124
Genes show as colored bars called features.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.903
A codon is three bases that code for one amino acid, a building block of protein.

00:00:44.903 --> 00:00:53.252
Choose Sequence, then Go to Location, type the position of the codon that changes in sickle cell disease, and click Go.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:55.848
The viewer zooms to three bases, GAG.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:02.626
The red row below the bases shows the amino acid each codon codes for.

00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:05.788
GAG codes for glutamic acid, written E.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.906
In sickle cell disease the middle A is a T.

00:01:09.906 --> 00:01:13.693
The codon becomes GTG, which codes for valine instead.

00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:19.654
The changed hemoglobin sticks together and bends red blood cells into a sickle shape.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:28.748
To mark the codon, drag across the three bases, choose Sequence, then Add Annotation, type a name, and click Add.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.982
LGE saves your annotation in the project.

00:01:33.982 --> 00:01:38.648
Go back to the codon and it appears on its own track, under the bases.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.700
Next, learn to judge a sequencing run, on the Videos page of the website.
